  "live_records": {
    "q-162415": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162415",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}},\\,t>-1.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})|}{{({1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{{(1+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{4}})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{\\sqrt{{[\\frac{(t+1)^{4}+1}{(t+1)^{4}}]}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{|(t+1)^{3}|\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{(t+1)^{3}\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
