    "q-162388": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162388",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+{2}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2{e}^{t}|}{{({e}^{2t}+4)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
