    "q-162419": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162419",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?",
      "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-2t,\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 2{e}^{2t}}{{((-2)^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{{(4+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{8{(1+{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
