    "q-162416": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162416",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?",
      "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
