      "problem": "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1-t)^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at $t=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1-t)^{2},\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|-2(1-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2|}{({(-2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t}|}{({(2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2(0){e}^{(0)}-4{e}^{(0)}|}{({(2(1-(0)))^{2}+({e}^{(0)})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
