    "q-162476": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162476",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
