    "q-34956": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34956",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot {e}^{t}-({e}^{t})\\cdot 0|}{{((-1)^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
