      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find the curvature of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi |}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1-0|}{{[1+(0+1)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
