    "q-162525": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162525",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
