    "q-34549": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34549",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
