    "q-162534": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162534",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
