    "q-162526": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162526",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-162526-a",
